**Verdict: partial.** The shared denominator permits \(\Theta(m\log m)\) conditioning loss at half the embeddings of a reduced linked pair. The same ternary basis witnesses the loss at all of them. Yet the family can be chosen so that every capped candidate lies on one \(k\)-line and contributes zero fresh higher-rank parity. The ideal-relative threshold inequality remains open. [Construction and proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:33)

### Proved — where the resultant bound stops

Write \(U=\prod_jR_j\). The cleared numerator of a ternary row is \(G_a=V_a+\sqrt2\,W_a\), where **both** integer polynomials have degree at most \(p+1\). Substituting \(P\) for \(\sqrt2\) gives a single integer polynomial but can raise its degree to \(n+p+1\). At embedding \(i\), \(t_i/M_i\) is the minimum, over ternary bases, of the largest normalized value \(\lvert G_a^\sigma(\alpha_i)\rvert/\max_j\lvert G_j^\sigma(\alpha_i)\rvert\) in that basis. The common denominator cancels exactly. [Numerator identity](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:4)

For a fixed row nonzero on the side, the product over its \(2n\) embeddings is the positive integer \(\lvert N_{k/\mathbf Q}\operatorname{Res}(P-\sqrt2,G_a)\rvert\). Bounding the other factors gives a uniform Liouville estimate, but its denominator contains the coefficient height of \(G_a\) to power \(2n-1\) and the Mahler measure of \(P^2-2\) to power \(p+1\). More directly, if one row loses at least \(\lambda\) at \(u\) embeddings across the two sides, then
\[
u\bigl(\lambda+\log(Cm)\bigr)\le H+D\log(Cm),
\qquad C=1+\sqrt2,\quad H=\sum_i\log M_i-\log J.
\]
At \(H\asymp Dm\log m\), this allows \(u\asymp D\). The resultant controls each fixed row’s product while allowing other conjugates to compensate. [Mahler estimate and counting bound](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:14)

The construction realizes that obstruction. Take \(q=m-2\) quadratic poles \(R_j=T^2-r_j^2\), so \(p=2m-4\). Interpolation makes the pole coordinates near zero approximate \(Sv\), where \(v=C_0^{-1}e_\ell\) is a column of an Alon–Vũ inverse. The rows of \(C_0\) evaluate there to at most \(2S\), while one generator has magnitude at least \(S\|v\|_\infty/2\). Adding a large multiple of \(T^{n/2}(T-1)U\) preserves the pole residues and the link at \(1\). Rouché’s theorem then puts half the roots for each sign and each side in that neighborhood. Consequently, at \(D/2\) embeddings,
\[
\log(M_i/t_i)\ge \tfrac12m\log m-O(m).
\]
Both sides are irreducible and their generator ideals are unit ideals. [Proof](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:42) · [Alon–Vũ, Main Theorem](https://web.math.princeton.edu/~nalon/PDFS/av1.pdf)

For any prescribed \(1\le Y<N^2\), the interpolation parameters can also force every nonscalar row above the two-sided norm cap. The exact surviving count is **six scalar units**, plus \(\pm\sqrt2\) when \(2^{2n}\le Y\). Thus large loss on many embeddings can coexist with zero fresh higher-rank yield. This construction uses unrestricted coefficient height. [Candidate count](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:74)

### Measured — all 195 registered pairs

I used one split semiprime at each of 60, 72, 84, 96 and 112 bits, every \(m=3,\ldots,8\), and every integer \(p=m,\ldots,2m\). The table uses all **9,096 absolute embeddings**, counting complex conjugates separately. Let \(\ell=\log(M_i/t_i)\) and \(r=\ell/(m\log m)\), since \(e=2\). The largest certified interval width was \(3\times10^{-9}\). Direct enumeration independently checked 12 embeddings. [Pre-registration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/prereg.txt:1) · [Every embedding](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/embeddings.csv:1) · [Certificate](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/measurement.txt:10)

| \(m\) | Embeddings | Median \(\ell\) | Median \(r\) | 90th pct. \(r\) | Max \(r\) | \(r\ge0.25\) | \(r\ge0.5\) | \(r\ge1\) |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 3 | 740 | 1.621 | .492 | 1.110 | 1.407 | 100.0% | 48.8% | 13.0% |
| 4 | 960 | 2.115 | .381 | .986 | 1.236 | 80.7% | 34.9% | 8.9% |
| 5 | 1,240 | 2.759 | .343 | .928 | 1.141 | 66.4% | 28.8% | 2.4% |
| 6 | 1,596 | 2.637 | .245 | .870 | 1.011 | 48.9% | 17.1% | 0.2% |
| 7 | 2,028 | 2.372 | .174 | .823 | .953 | 32.8% | 13.3% | 0% |
| 8 | 2,532 | 2.280 | .137 | .633 | .890 | 19.7% | 10.4% | 0% |

The normalized median fell at every rank. The upper tail persisted at \(m=8\), where **33 of 45 pairs** had at least one embedding above half the benchmark. Real embeddings accounted for 212 of the 214 observations above the full benchmark. Values above one are compatible with the \(O_k(m)\) term in the Cramer bound. These frequencies describe the registered balanced-digit fixtures. [Distribution by rank and embedding type](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/summary.csv:2) · [Pair-level results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/cell_summary.csv:152)

**Exponent ledger and next step.** At the proposed \(L[1/4]\) balance, \(x/T\) and \(Dm\log m\) both have size \(x^{3/4+o(1)}\), against a target log norm of \(x^{1/2+o(1)}\). KH retains the generator-height exponent \(c=1\), giving the model’s \(1/3\) barrier there. The next proof target is the necessary range \(H=O(x/T)\): bound the contribution to \(\sum_i\log t_i\) from the conjugates that compensate for the local losses. A height-uniform exclusion of those losses is now refuted. [Ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/dd_c/proof.txt:104) · [Necessary height range](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/experiments/chase/ch_L2c/proof.txt:42)